<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  An icon-only chrome control at rest: Display, Objects, Hide UI, the inspector
  toggle, the restore chip.

  Borderless until touched. These controls sit in a row on an already-opaque
  toolbar, and giving each of them its own filled, stroked box drew three or
  four competing rectangles across the top of the model for glyphs that are
  perfectly legible on the strip itself. The box was carrying no information:
  the surface underneath already says "this is chrome", and the icon already
  says what the control does.

  It stays a state list rather than becoming a null background, because the
  pressed answer is the part that matters — a control that looks dead until
  native code replies is the defect the whole state-list mechanism exists to
  prevent. The ACTIVE form is bg_capsule_control_active, so a control whose panel
  is open is the only one in the row wearing a shape, which is what makes "open"
  readable at a glance.

  The corner is radius_control_inset, not radius_control. Every icon control in
  the product sits inside a floating capsule — Display and Hide UI in the utility
  group, the precision toggle in its own, the `+` in the Objects capsule — and a
  10 dp box painted flush inside a 26 dp capsule leaves a crescent of capsule
  showing beside it. The lone exception, the restore chip in the overlay, wants a
  pill anyway.
-->
<selector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:state_pressed="true">
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="?attr/fsControlSurfacePressed" />
            <corners android:radius="@dimen/radius_control_inset" />
        </shape>
    </item>
    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
            <corners android:radius="@dimen/radius_control_inset" />
        </shape>
    </item>
</selector>
